RUNWAY NOTES
Thursday, February 26, 2026

Burn multiple is a lagging indicator

Every board deck I have seen this quarter has a burn multiple on it, and every one presents it as a health metric. It is not. It is an autopsy metric. Net burn divided by net new ARR tells you, precisely and too late, how efficient the last two quarters were. By the time it moves, the hiring decisions that moved it are nine months old.

The leading indicators are less tidy. Three I watch:

Sales capacity ramp. How many quota-carrying reps are fully ramped versus hired? A company with six reps, two ramped, has a burn multiple that looks terrible today and will look fine in two quarters if the ramp works. If the ramp does not work, no metric on the deck will save you.

Pipeline created per dollar of marketing and SDR spend, trailing 90 days. This moves a quarter before bookings and two quarters before burn multiple. When it turns down, the fix is cheap; when burn multiple turns, the fix is layoffs.

Gross margin trajectory, not level. A 68% gross margin that was 61% two quarters ago is a company learning to deliver. A 78% margin that was 84% is a company that started doing services to close deals and has not told the board yet.

Benchmarks, because you will ask: median burn multiple for Series A software companies in our dataset is 1.9x, top quartile under 1.2x. Companies below $5M ARR with a multiple above 3x raised their next round 40% of the time; above 2x but under 3x, 71%. The line is real but it is a line drawn through the past.

Put burn multiple on the deck. Put the three above in front of it.

